  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a sharp spike followed by volatility (e.g., Example 2-4), the model systematically underestimates the immediate post-spike mean reversion and fails to capture the subsequent crash, resulting in a persistent upward bias.",
    "The model exhibits a strong lag in reacting to negative sentiment spikes (e.g., -0.25 or -0.20); forecasts remain flat or slowly rising while true outcomes drop significantly once sentiment turns negative.",
    "In low-volatility consolidation periods with neutral sentiment (Example 1), the model correctly identifies a range-bound mean but fails to account for sudden volatility expansion that causes prices to breach forecast bands.",
    "When sentiment history shows mixed signals (positive and negative) within a short window, the model's linear extrapolation ignores the high probability of mean reversion toward historical averages rather than continuing the trend.",
    "The 30-day horizon consistently overestimates price stability; true outcomes in these periods show much higher variance and frequent reversals compared to the smooth forecast curves."
  ]
}
